# Clean Code: Comments

**A comment is a failure to express intent in code.** The only truly good comment is the one you found a way not to write. Comments drift out of date and lie; code is the only reliable truth.

## Prefer code over comments

```java
// BAD
// Check to see if the employee is eligible for full benefits
if ((employee.flags & HOURLY_FLAG) && (employee.age > 65))

// GOOD
if (employee.isEligibleForFullBenefits())
```

## Good comments (use sparingly, keep accurate)
- **Legal** headers (keep short; link to license).
- **Informative** facts a name can't convey (prefer renaming first).
- **Explanation of intent** — the "why" behind a non-obvious decision.
- **Clarification** of un-renameable external API values (`// a < b`) — verify carefully.
- **Warning of consequences** (`// not thread safe`, `// don't run unless you have time to kill`).
- **TODO** for genuinely deferred work (review/remove regularly).
- **Amplification** of a line whose importance isn't obvious.
- **Javadoc for public APIs** (apply the same discipline).

## Bad comments (delete / avoid)
- Mumbling, redundant restatements (`i++; // increment i`), misleading wording, mandated comments on everything.
- Journal/change-log comments, attributions/bylines, position-marker banners, closing-brace comments — SCM and small functions replace these.
- **Commented-out code (C5): delete on sight** — SCM remembers it.
- HTML in comments, nonlocal info, too much info, inobvious connections, noise Javadoc on obvious fields.
- Function-header comments on small functions; Javadoc on non-public internals.

## Heuristics
- **C1** No metadata/history/authors in comments (use SCM/tracker).
- **C2** Update or delete obsolete comments immediately.
- **C3** No redundant comments.
- **C4** If worth writing, write it well — brief, correct grammar, no rambling.
- **C5** Delete commented-out code.
